## Handling DOM Events in Vue.js

Vue provides the **`v-on` directive** to listen for DOM events on an element and execute JavaScript when they are triggered.

### Basic Syntax

The core syntax is `v-on:event-name="handler"`. The `handler` can be either an inline statement or a method name.

```html
<!-- Listen for a click event -->
<button v-on:click="..."></button>

<!-- Listen for a mouse movement event -->
<div v-on:mousemove="..."></div>
```

### Event Handlers

There are two primary ways to handle an event:

1.  **Inline Handlers**: For very simple logic, you can write JavaScript directly in the HTML attribute. The code has access to the component's data and methods.

    ```html
    <button v-on:click="age++">Add a Year</button>
    <button v-on:click="age--">Subtract a Year</button>
    ```

2.  **Method Handlers**: For more complex logic, it's best to bind the event to a method defined in the `methods` object of your Vue instance. This keeps your templates clean.

    ```javascript
    // In your Vue instance
    // ...
    methods: {
      add() {
        this.age++;
      }
    }
    ```

    ```html
    <!-- Template calls the method -->
    <button v-on:click="add">Add a Year</button>
    ```

> [!NOTE]
> When binding directly to a method name without passing arguments (e.g., `v-on:click="add"`), you can omit the parentheses `()`. Vue automatically knows to call the method when the event occurs.

### Passing Parameters to Methods

You can pass custom arguments to your methods from the template. When you do this, you must include the parentheses `()`.

```html
<!-- Pass a number to the method -->
<button v-on:dblclick="add(10)">Add 10 Years</button>
```

```javascript
// The method now accepts a parameter
methods: {
  add(increment) {
    this.age += increment;
  }
}
```

### The Native Event Object

When a method is used as an event handler, Vue automatically passes the native DOM event object as the first argument.

```javascript
methods: {
  updateXY(event) {
    // We can access properties of the event object
    console.log(event.offsetX, event.offsetY);
    this.x = event.offsetX;
    this.y = event.offsetY;
  }
}
```

### Shorthand Syntax

Vue offers a convenient shorthand for `v-on:` using the **at-symbol (`@`)**.

```html
<!-- Standard syntax -->
<button v-on:click="add"></button>

<!-- Shorthand syntax -->
<button @click="add"></button>
```
